This, over the years, is an incredibly common
problem in software enterprises: the best coder in
the house is often also the house prima donna; this
goes back to the days when software development was
called an "art" instead of recognized as something
struggling painfully to become a "science".

Both the slowly building realization that software
development of code expected to have a long
maintenance segment for that code, in which features
are added and bugs are removed, cannot afford to pay
the price of prima donnas, and the slow emergence of
objective standards of _group_ software development
efficiency, as opposed to standards focused on the
"productivity" of the individual

  ["How many lines of fully documented, debugged,
  fully tested, integrated and installed code did
  Kent deliver today" has had answers ranging from
  600 lines, one instance (long, long day), to only
  a fraction of a single line ("I've isolated the
  problem to these three modules and these five
  possible causes") in many, many instances, and yet
  the happiness of my competent supervisors has
  always been greater in the latter cases.]

such as the Software Engineering Institute's five
level Capability Maturity Model, the most respected
of these standards,


http://www.sei.cmu.edu/publications/books/process/cmm-improving-sw-process.html

and other, often government or industry imposed
"software-goodness" standards,

[the extent of industry and government interest in
software engineering standards is indicated in this
somewhat old link]

http://csdl.computer.org/comp/proceedings/isess/1997/7837/00/7837toc.htm

have moved software engineering from a model of
treasuring the prima donnas to a model of putting
them on short notice that their services might be
better employed elsewhere if they are unable to
think outside the box provided by the display in
front of them.

Folks who do software for a living, but hate to
type, are advised to set up in business for
themselves, and many do.

The industry is no longer so hungry for employees as
before the dot com bubble burst of late 1999 that it
tolerates such prima donna habits at the "software
in the large" levels in big enterprises at all, and
that intolerance is moving to smaller enterprises
rapidly as the documentation of the cost benefits of
programming _for the needs of the group_ at CMM
levels 3 and up become more widely published:

http://itresearch.forbes.com/detail/RES/963410535_141.html

